6,T7]), so T7 is isolated.\n- B6: could go with B4,B5 or B7,B8 \u2014 B7,B8 are in previous player\u2019s meld [B7,B8,B9], so B6 is risky.\n- F2: wind tile, no pung yet; others have played many F tiles, but F2 hasn't been seen much.\n- W5 is fully used up (0 left), so no one can draw it.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Discarding T7: T7 appears safe \u2014 across player already completed [T5,T6,T7], so unlikely they need another T7. But T7 could help someone else's hand subtly.\n- Discarding B6: B6 is dangerous? Previous player has [B7,B8,B9] and [B1,B2,B3], suggesting a possible middle gap. If they are going for Full Flush or All Kinds, B6 might complete a sequence. But B6 is not adjacent to their known melds.\n- Discarding F2: F2 is a South Wind? No \u2014 F2 is South Wind? Wait: F1=East, F2=South, F3=West, F4=North. So F2 is South Wind \u2014 same as Seat and Prevailing Wind! But I already have a pung of F4 (North Wind), not F2. Mistake!\n\nWait: F4 is North Wind. But Seat Wind and Prevailing Wind are **South Wind**, which is F2.\n\nOh! Critical insight: I have a pung of **F4 (North Wind)**, but the beneficial wind is **F2 (South Wind)**. So my F4 pung gives me 2 points for Pung of Honors, but *not* for Prevalent or Seat Wind. If I held F2, I could use it for that.\n\nBut I only have one F2 in hand. If I can form a pung of F2, that would give me +2 for Dragon/Prevalent/Seat Wind patterns.\n\nBut others have discarded many F tiles: history shows Play F3, F3, F3, F4, F4 (by me), F1, F1, F1 \u2014 but almost no F2 played. Unshown tiles show F2: 4 copies still unshown \u2014 meaning none have been discarded or revealed. That means all four F2 tiles are still live.\n\nTherefore, F2 is extremely rare and valuable \u2014 possibly someone is waiting on it.\n\nSo discarding F2 risks feeding a winning hand.\n\nNow consider scoring:\n\nCurrent Melds:\n- [W2,W3,W4]: Chow\n- [B3,B4,B5]: Chow\n- [F4,F4,F4]: Pung of North Wind \u2192 2 points (Pung of Terminals/Honors)\n\nHand: T7, B3, B4, B6, F2\n\nWait \u2014 this totals 5 tiles. After drawing, I should have 14 tiles total: 3 melds \u00d7 3 = 9 tiles, plus 5 singles = 14. Then I must discard one.\n\nCan I win?\n\nDo I have a pair? Not obviously. Possible pairs: B3-B3? I have one B3 in hand, and one was used in [B3,B4,B5] \u2014 but that doesn\u2019t mean I have two B3s unless I drew another. Similarly for B4.\n\nLet\u2019s count actual tile counts.\n\nFrom meld [B3,B4,B5], I used one B3, one B4, one B5.\n\nIn hand: B3, B4, B6, T7, F2 \u2192 so yes, I have second copies of B3 and B4.\n\nSo possible pairs: B3 pair? B4 pair?\n\nBut I can only keep one.\n\nIf I keep B3 and discard B4, I have a pair of B3? Only if I have two B3s \u2014 yes: one in meld, one in hand \u2192 no, the meld uses one B3, hand has one \u2192 total two B3s.\n\nSame for B4.\n\nSo I could treat B3 or B4 as potential pair.\n\nBut I also have B6 and T7 \u2014 both single suited tiles.\n\nTo win, I need four melds and a pair.\n\nCurrently three melds.\n\nNeed one more meld and a defined pair.\n\nPossible fourth meld:\n- Use T7: needs T5-T6 or T8-T9 \u2014 T5,T6 are gone (in other players\u2019 hands), T8,T9: T9 is 0 left \u2014 all discarded or used. So T7 cannot form chow. No other T7s seen \u2014 I have one, others haven't shown multiple \u2014 so pung unlikely.\n- Use B6: could form B6,B7,B8 \u2014 but B7,B8 are in [B7,B8,B9] (previous player) \u2014 so likely safe.\n- Use B3: with B1,B2 or B4,B5 \u2014 B1,B2 are in [B1,B2,B3] (same player), so B3 won\u2019t help. B4,B5 available? I have B4, B5 used in my own meld \u2014 so no.\n- Use B4: similar issue.\n- Use F2: could form pung if I had three \u2014 I only have one.\n\nSo no immediate win unless I draw a very specific tile.\n\nThus, I must discard.\n\nNow evaluate each option:\n\n1. **Play F2**: High risk. F2 is South Wind \u2014 both prevalent and seat wind. Any player needing a wind pung could win off this. Since no F2 has been played yet (all 4 still hidden), it's likely someone is collecting them. Discarding F2 could feed a winning hand (e.g., someone with two F2s already). Avoid.\n\n2. **Play T7**: T7 \u2014 across player has [T5,T6,T7], so they\u2019ve completed that chow. They discarded T8, T9, T1 \u2014 so unlikely holding T7s. Next player has [T4,T5,T6], discarded T9 \u2014 so T7 less useful. B9 is exhausted (0 left), so no one can use T7 for knitted straight easily. T7 seems relatively safe.\n\n3. **Play B6**: B6 \u2014 previous player has [B7,B8,B9] and [B1,B2,B3], suggesting a possible Middle Tiles or Full Flush in Bamboo? But missing middle. B6 could help form B4,B5,B6 or B5,B6,B7 \u2014 B5 is used in my meld, B7 in theirs. But B6 alone isn't completing anything obvious. Still, it's central \u2014 higher utility than edge tiles.\n\n4. **Play B3**: I already used B3 in [B3,B4,B5]. There are 2 B3s unshown. Previous player has [B1,B2,B3] \u2014 so they had one B3. Could they want another? Possibly for pair or another chow. But they already completed the chow, so maybe not. Still, B3 is terminal \u2014 could be part of Outside Hand. Risky.\n\n5. **Play B4**: Similar \u2014 B4 is in my own meld, and there are 3 B4s unshown. Could be useful for chows like B2,B3,B4 or B4,B5,B6. But B2,B3 are in previous player\u2019s hand \u2014 they have [B1,B2,B3], so likely done. B4,B5,B6 \u2014 possible, but B5 is in my meld, so only one left? Unshown B5: 3 \u2014 so plenty left. But no evidence anyone is building that.\n\nAmong all, **F2** is most dangerous due to wind value.\n\n**T7** seems safest \u2014 high number, not involved in any major pattern (T9 gone), and opponent has already completed T-based chows.\n\nAlso, looking at scoring potential:\n\nIf I can form a Half Flush or Full Flush? I have two chows in different suits \u2014 W and B \u2014 so not flush.\n\nBut if I keep B3/B4/B6, I'm leaning toward Bamboo suit.\n\nBut I also have T7 \u2014 third suit.\n\nSo likely **All Types** (8 points) is achievable.\n\nAlso, I have honor meld (F4 pung) \u2014 so honors present.\n\nWith Seat Wind (F2) potentially in hand, if I kept it, I could aim for **Mixed Double Pung** or **Prevalent Wind** if I ever got more F2s \u2014 but I only have one.\n\nNo chance of getting a pung of F2 unless I draw three \u2014 impossible.\n\nSo keeping F2 only helps for pair (unlikely win condition) or for defense.\n\nBut discarding it risks everything.\n\nThus, better to discard a suited tile.\n\nBetween B3, B4, B6, T7:\n\n- B3 and B4 are terminals \u2014 discarding them risks **Outside Hand** or **Reversible Tiles**?\n- Reversible Tiles include B3, B4, B5, B9, T2, T4, T5, T6, T8, T9, J3 \u2014 so B3, B4 qualify.\n- But B9 is exhausted, so hard to build full reversible.\n- Still, possible.\n\nT7 is not in Reversible list (shape not symmetric), so safer.\n\nFinal decision:\n\nDiscard **T7** \u2014 it's a simple, non-terminal, non-honor, and opponents have already used surrounding tiles. Least dangerous.\n\nAnswer: Play T7\n\nReason: T7 is surrounded by melds already completed by other players ([T5,T6,T7] by across player), making it highly unlikely anyone is waiting on T7. It does not contribute to knitted patterns or reversible sets significantly. Discarding F2 (South Wind) would be extremely risky as it could complete a prevalent/seat wind pung for an opponent. Among the options, T7 poses the lowest danger.\n\nSituation: I hold three melds and five single tiles: T7, B3, B4, B6, F2. One of these must be discarded. Opponents have exposed key sequences in all suits, and wind F2 remains completely unseen, indicating high risk.\nEvaluation: F2 is dangerous to discard due to its status as Prevailing and Seat Wind. B3, B4, and B6 are involved in bamboo sequences that others may be pursuing. T7 is least critical, as the corresponding chow is already completed by another player.\nReason: T7 is the safest discard because it is unlikely to complete an opponent's winning hand, given it has already been used in an exposed meld by the player across.\nAnswer: Play T7"}
