3: formed [W2,W3,W4] using discarded W3 \u2192 I had W2,W4 in hand\n- Peng F4: formed [F4,F4,F4] using someone\u2019s F4, then added own F4 \u2192 later promoted\n- Chi B4: formed [B3,B4,B5] using discarded B4 \u2192 I had B3,B5\n- BuGang F4: added fourth F4 to existing pung \u2192 made kong \u2192 drew replacement tile\n\nAfter BuGang F4, I drew a tile (replacement), then must discard one.\n\nCurrent hand: Before drawing, I had 13 tiles: the three melds account for 3+3+3=9 tiles, plus 4 singles. After drawing replacement (14th tile), I now have 5 single tiles, from which I must discard one.\n\nSo current single tiles: [W6, B3, B4, B6, F2] \u2014 these are the five candidates to discard.\n\nNow evaluate what I'm waiting on.\n\nI have:\n- Meld 1: [W2,W3,W4] \u2014 chow\n- Meld 2: [F4\u00d74] \u2014 kong\n- Meld 3: [B3,B4,B5] \u2014 chow\n- Single tiles: W6, B3, B4, B6, F2\n\nWait \u2014 I already used B3 and B4 in the chow [B3,B4,B5]. Can I still have them in hand?\n\nOnly if I have duplicates. Let's check availability:\n\nB3: total 4  \nUsed:  \n- Me: [B3,B4,B5] \u2192 1  \n- Previous player: [B1,B2,B3] \u2192 1  \n\u2192 2 used, 2 left  \nUnshown: 2 \u2192 ok  \nSo I could have another B3 in hand? But I only had one to begin with.\n\nBut when I formed [B3,B4,B5] via Chi B4, I must have discarded something else afterward. The sequence: after Chi B4, I played T4, T6, etc. So likely, the B3 and B5 were consumed in the meld. Unless I had two B3s.\n\nBut given unshown B3: 2, and two used in other melds, yes, two remain \u2192 possible I still have one.\n\nBut more likely: the system lists current hand tiles excluding those in melds. So perhaps the [B3, B4] listed in hand are *different instances*, but that leads to inconsistency.\n\nAlternative interpretation: Maybe the chow [B3,B4,B5] is not yet formed? But history says \"Chi B4\", so it is.\n\nBest resolution: Accept the state as given \u2014 current unmelded tiles are [W6, B3, B4, B6, F2], despite potential overlap. Possibly the system allows listing all holdings.\n\nBut logically, once a tile is melded, it's fixed. So likely, the \"hand tiles\" here refer to current loose tiles, and B3/B4 listed are additional copies.\n\nAssume I have extra B3 and B4.\n\nSo current live draws:\n- I need to complete a pair or another meld.\n\nPossible waits:\n- Pair possibilities: F2 (South Wind) \u2014 I have one, others have none shown? F2 unshown: 4 \u2192 all four still available? But F2 is South Wind, prevalent and seat wind.\n\nWait: unshown F2: 4 \u2192 yes, no F2 discarded or melded \u2192 so all four are still in play.\n\nI hold one F2 \u2192 if I keep it, I can try to make a pair if I draw another F2.\n\nOther singles: W6, B3, B4, B6\n\nAlso, W6: unshown 1 \u2192 only one left\n\nB3: unshown 2 \u2192 two left\n\nB4: unshown 3 \u2192 three left\n\nB6: unshown 3 \u2192 three left\n\nBut previous player has multiple bamboo melds: [B1,B2,B3], [B6,B7,B8], [B7,B8,B9] \u2014 so they've used B6, B7, B8, B9 heavily.\n\nThey have [B6,B7,B8] \u2192 used one B6\n\nSo B6: total 4 \u2192 one used \u2192 3 unshown \u2192 ok\n\nCan I make a winning hand?\n\nCurrent structure: 3 melds + 5 singles \u2192 after discard, 4 melds + pair? No, I already have 3 melds (one being kong), and after BuGang I drew, so now I have 3 melds + 5 singles \u2192 need to discard one to go to 14.\n\nTo win, I need four melds and a pair.\n\nCurrently, I have three melds \u2192 need one more meld and a pair.\n\nSo I must either:\n- Draw a tile that forms a chow/pung with two of my singles and completes a pair\n- Win by claiming a discard\n\nBut I cannot declare win now; I must discard first.\n\nSo choice is which tile to discard to maximize safety and readiness.\n\nEvaluate valid actions: [Play F2; Play B6; Play B3; Play B4; Play W6]\n\nOption 1: Discard F2 (South Wind)\n- Pros: It's a wind tile, but I have no other F2, and winds are dangerous because others might be collecting.\n- Cons: Since I am South seat and prevailing is South, F2 is valuable. Pung of F2 gives 2 points (Prevalent Wind + Seat Wind). If I keep it, I might draw another and make a pair, then wait on third.\n- However, unshown F2: 4 \u2192 all exist \u2192 very unlikely anyone has a pair; probably safe.\n- But discarding it wastes a chance to build toward a strong pattern.\n\nOption 2: Discard W6\n- W6: only one unshown \u2192 three already out?\n- Who has W6? Next player played W6, across played W6, previous played W6 \u2192 at least three discarded \u2192 only one left \u2192 high chance someone will want it for chow [W5,W6,W7] or [W6,W7,W8]\n- But W5: unshown 0 \u2192 all gone \u2192 so no one can make [W5,W6,W7] anymore\n- W7: unshown 2 \u2192 two left\n- But without W5, W6 can only be used in [W6,W7,W8] \u2014 requires W7 and W8\n- W8: unshown 3 \u2192 plenty\n- But risky \u2014 possible someone is waiting on W6\n- Also, I might use W6 in future chow, but I have no W7 or W8 \u2192 unlikely\n\nOption 3: Discard B3\n- B3: unshown 2 \u2192 two left\n- Previous player has [B1,B2,B3] and [B6,B7,B8] \u2014 they may have completed hand\n- They also have [B7,B8,B9] \u2014 so their bamboo is upper end\n- B3 might be safe\n- But I could use B3 in [B2,B3,B4] or [B3,B4,B5] \u2014 but I already have [B3,B4,B5] melded \u2192 so duplicate B3 can only form pung\n- Possible, but low utility\n\nOption 4: Discard B4\n- Similar to B3\n- I already have [B3,B4,B5] \u2192 so another B4 could help make pung\n- But unshown B4: 3 \u2192 many left\n- Previous player doesn't have B4 in their melds \u2192 might be safe\n- But I could aim for All Simples or Full Flush?\n\nWait \u2014 consider hand composition.\n\nCurrent melds:\n- [W2,W3,W4] \u2014 simples, middle\n- [F4\u00d74] \u2014 honor\n- [B3,B4,B5] \u2014 simples\n\nSingles: W6 (simple), B3 (simple), B4 (simple), B6 (simple), F2 (wind)\n\nNo terminals or honors except F2 and F4 (already in kong)\n\nF4 is North Wind \u2014 not prevalent or seat wind \u2192 so its pung is just Dragon Pung equivalent (but wind)\n\nBut I have F2 \u2014 South Wind \u2014 which is both prevalent and seat wind.\n\nIf I can make a pair of F2, I get:\n- Seat Wind (2 pts)\n- Prevalent Wind (2 pts)\n- And if I win off a discard, Concealed Hand broken, but if self-drawn, Fully Concealed Hand (4 pts)\n\nBut I have many exposed melds \u2192 not concealed.\n\nCurrent hand has:\n- One kong (exposed via Peng + BuGang) \u2192 so not concealed\n- Two chows via Chi \u2192 exposed\n\u2192 So hand is fully exposed \u2192 no chance for Fully Concealed or Concealed Hand\n\nThus, Self Drawn (1 pt) is possible, but not Fully Concealed (4) or Concealed Hand (2).\n\nCan I go for a value hand?\n\nOptions:\n- Full Flush? No \u2014 I have characters and bamboos and winds \u2192 mixed suits\n- Half Flush? Only if one suit dominates \u2014 but I have W and B and F \u2192 no\n- All Simples? Yes! All my melds and singles are number tiles 2\u20136, no 1/9 or dragons.\n    - Except: F4 and F2 are winds \u2192 so not All Simples\n    - F4 kong breaks it\n- So cannot have All Simples\n\nBut maybe I can change direction?\n\nNo \u2014 I\u2019m stuck with F4 kong.\n\nAlternatively, focus on making another pung.\n\nBest bet: try to make a pair of F2, since it scores 4 points if I complete it (Seat + Prevalent Wind).\n\nF2 unshown: 4 \u2192 all available \u2192 so very hard to get a pair.\n\nAlternatively, discard less useful tile.\n\nCompare discards:\n\n- W6: only one left \u2192 high risk of being claimed for chow [W6,W7,W8] \u2014 someone may be waiting on it. Dangerous.\n- B3: possible for [B2,B3,B4] or [B3,B4,B5] \u2014 but B2 unshown: 1, B4: 3, B5: 3 \u2192 possible, but previous player already has B3 in meld, and they might be done.\n- B4: similar\n- B6: B6,B7,B8 \u2014 previous player has two such melds \u2192 they might be going for Four Pure Shifted Chows or similar \u2192 very dangerous to discard B6\n    - They have [B6,B7,B8] already \u2192 if they have another B6, they might be waiting on B5 or B9\n    - Or they might want B6 to make pung\n    - But they played B7 twice \u2192 might be discarding excess\n    - Still, B6 is risky\n\n- F2: safest? Because:\n    - It's a wind tile\n    - No one has shown interest in F2\n    - Prevailing is South, so players might collect F2, but no one has punged\n    - Across player played F3, F1 \u2014 avoided F2\n    - Next played F3, F4 \u2014 no F2\n    - Previous played F3 \u2014 no F2\n    - So likely no one is building F2\n\nMoreover, F2 is valuable to me \u2014 if I keep it, I can hope to draw another for pair.\n\nBut I must discard one now.\n\nAmong the options, B3 and B4 are duplicated in my hand and partially in melds \u2014 but since I already have a chow using B3/B4, extra ones can only form pung.\n\nBut chances are low.\n\nW6 is very dangerous due to last one.\n\nB6 is dangerous because previous player has multiple B6-related melds.\n\nSo best discard is F2 \u2014 even though it's wind, it's less likely to be needed, and keeping three weak simples may not help.\n\nBut wait \u2014 I have only one F2. If I discard it, I lose chance for pair.\n\nWhereas W6, B3, B4, B6 are all suited and could be part of future chows.\n\nBut I already have four melds conceptually? No \u2014 I have three melds, need one more.\n\nAfter discarding, I\u2019ll have three melds and four singles \u2014 then I need to draw into fourth meld or pair.\n\nBetter to keep F2 as potential pair tile.\n\nThen among the suited tiles, which is safest?\n\nW6: only one left \u2192 very likely to be called for [W6,W7,W8] \u2014 avoid\n\nB6: previous player has [B6,B7,B8] and [B7,B8,B9] \u2014 they might be waiting on B6 for pung or another chow \u2014 dangerous\n\nB3: previous player has [B1,B2,B3] \u2014 might want another B3 for pung \u2014 possible\n\nB4: no one has shown B4 \u2014 next player has T4,T5,T6 \u2014 not related\nAcross has T5,T6,T7 \u2014 no B\nPrevious has B1,B2,B3 \u2014 no B4\nSo B4 might be safer\n\nAlso, unshown B4: 3 \u2014 many left\n\nSimilarly, B3 unshown: 2 \u2014 also many\n\nBut previous player has B3 in meld \u2014 might want to kong\n\nThey played B3? No \u2014 they formed [B1,B2,B3] via Chi B3? History: \"Chi B2\" and \"Chi B7\" \u2014 actually, \"Chi B2\": formed [B1,B2,B3]? Could be.\n\n\"Chi B2\" \u2014 means they completed [B1,B2,B3] by taking B2 \u2014 so they had B1,B3\n\nThen later played B3? No \u2014 they played B5, B8, B9, etc.\n\nNo sign of discarding B3 \u2014 so they may still want to kong B3\n\nSo discard B3 is dangerous\n\nB4: no one has shown interest \u2014 safer\n\nW6: dangerous\n\nB6: dangerous\n\nF2: safe, but valuable\n\nSo trade-off: keep F2, discard B4?\n\nBut B4 might be usable in [B4,B5,B6] \u2014 I have B5 in meld, B6 in hand \u2014 so if I keep B4 and B6, and draw B5, I could form chow \u2014 but B5 is in my existing meld [B3,B4,B5] \u2014 unless I have extra\n\nUnlikely.\n\nNo strong internal coordination.\n\nBest strategy: minimize danger.\n\nLeast likely to be claimed: F2 or B4\n\nBut F2 gives potential scoring \u2014 if I keep it, I have a chance at pair and 4 points (Seat + Prevalent Wind)\n\nWhereas B4 has no special value\n\nSo better to keep F2\n\nThen discard B4\n\nBut is B4 safe?\n\nWho might want B4?\n\n- For [B2,B3,B4]: B2 unshown: 1, B3: 2 \u2014 possible\n- For [B3,B4,B5]: B3,B4,B5 \u2014 B5 unshown: 3 \u2014 possible\n- For [B4,B5,B6]: B5,B6 \u2014 possible\n\nBut no player has shown bamboo below B6 except previous who has B1,B2,B3 \u2014 so they might not want B4\n\nNext player: T4,T5,T6 \u2014 might want B4 for knitted? Unlikely\n\nSo B4 is relatively safe\n\nAlternatively, W6 is extremely dangerous \u2014 only one left, and W7,W8 available\n\nSomeone is almost certainly waiting on W6 for [W6,W7,W8]\n\nSimilarly, B6: previous player has two B6,B7,B8 patterns \u2014 very likely they want B6\n\nSo discard B4 or F2\n\nFinal decision: Keep F2 for potential scoring, discard B4 as it's less critical\n\nAnswer: Play B4\n\nBut wait \u2014 valid actions include Play B4\n\nYes.\n\nReason: B4 is relatively safe to discard given lack of visible interest from opponents, while F2 offers potential for scoring with Seat and Prevalent Wind if a pair can be formed. W6, B6, and B3 are riskier discards due to possible chow or pung completions by the previous player or others.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Play W6: High risk \u2014 likely to be claimed for [W6,W7,W8]\n- Play B6: High risk \u2014 previous player has multiple B6,B7,B8 melds\n- Play B3: Medium risk \u2014 previous player has [B1,B2,B3], may want to kong\n- Play F2: Low risk, but loses potential 4-point pattern\n- Play B4: Lowest risk among suited, keeps F2 for scoring\n\nThus, best balance.\n\nSituation: I have three exposed melds ([W2,W3,W4], [F4\u00d74], [B3,B4,B5]) and five single tiles: [W6, B3, B4, B6, F2]. I must discard one. Opponents have exposed several bamboo and character melds, with the previous player showing strong interest in mid-upper bamboos.\nEvaluation: Discarding W6 or B6 is highly dangerous due to potential chow completions. B3 risks completing a pung for the previous player. F2 is safe but valuable for scoring. B4 is relatively safe and less critical.\nReason: B4 poses minimal risk of being claimed while preserving the chance to score with a pair of South Winds (F2), which would yield 4 points (Seat Wind and Prevalent Wind).\nAnswer: Play B4"}
