, **I cannot win unless I form a pair**. So I must discard one tile and hope to draw the duplicate of one of the remaining four.\n\nHowever, **none of my current five tiles can form a pair unless drawn again**, so I must discard one that is least likely to help me or most dangerous to keep.\n\nNow consider scoring patterns:\n- I have a kong of F4 (South Wind), which counts for **Prevalent Wind (2 pts)** and **Seat Wind (2 pts)** \u2014 valuable.\n- My melds are mixed suit chows; no chance for All Simples (due to W2/W3/W4), Full Flush, or major patterns.\n- But I might aim for minimal 8-point win.\n\nPossible patterns I could reach:\n- **All Chows (2 pts)** \u2014 yes, if I end with four chows and a pair \u2192 valid.\n- **Melded Hand (6 pts)** \u2014 yes, because all my melds are exposed (Chow, Kong via Peng/BuGang), and if I claim the winning tile from another player \u2192 possible.\n- **Self Drawn (1 pt)** \u2014 only if I draw the winning tile myself.\n- **One Voided Suit?** I have W, T, B \u2014 all three suits present \u2192 no.\n- **No Honors?** I have F2 \u2192 no.\n- **Dragon Pung?** No dragon melds.\n- But I do have **Seat Wind & Prevalent Wind pung/kong**: F4\u00d74 \u2192 2+2 = 4 points already.\n- If I complete the hand with all exposed melds and win by claiming, I get Melded Hand (6) + Seat Wind (2) + Prevalent Wind (2) = 10 points \u2014 sufficient.\n- Alternatively, if I self-draw and have concealed wait, maybe Fully Concealed Hand? No \u2014 I have exposed melds.\n\nSo best path: **Aim for Melded Hand (6) + Seat Wind (2) + Prevalent Wind (2) = 10 pts**, or possibly more.\n\nNow evaluate each valid action:\n\n**Play F2**: Discard F2. Then my hand becomes [W3, T8, B3, B6]. To win, I need to draw a duplicate of one of these. F2 would no longer be in hand, so lose potential pair. But F2 is a wind tile \u2014 pairing it gives nothing special beyond pair value. However, keeping F2 allows possibility of **pair of F2**, which supports Melded Hand pattern. Since two F2 remain unshown, it's possible someone discards it. But discarding F2 removes that path. Risky.\n\n**Play B6**: B6 is dangerous \u2014 previous player has [B6,B7,B8] meld, and they just played B7 \u2014 possibly completing their hand. Only one B6 left unshown. Unlikely to draw. Also, B6 cannot form any strong sequence now. Low utility.\n\n**Play W3**: Already used W3 in [W2,W3,W4], so another W3 could make a pair. One W3 unshown. Possible, but risky \u2014 W3 is not common.\n\n**Play B3**: Already used in [B3,B4,B5], similar to W3. One B3 unshown. Could pair, but low probability.\n\n**Play T8**: Two T8 unshown. No one has shown T8 in melds except next player discarded T8 earlier. Still, two remain \u2014 relatively safe to keep. High chance of being able to pair later.\n\nThus, among the five, **T8 and F2 are the safest to keep**, as two copies remain. **B6, W3, B3** have only one copy left \u2014 harder to complete pair.\n\nBut we must discard one.\n\nBest move is to **discard the tile least likely to help us complete a pair and least useful**.\n\n- B6: only one left, and opponent has already used it in a meld \u2192 unlikely to come back.\n- Similarly, W3 and B3: only one left each.\n- T8: two left \u2014 good chance.\n- F2: two left \u2014 also good.\n\nSo eliminate **B6, W3, B3** as better to keep than discard? Wait \u2014 no: since we can only keep one pair candidate, we should discard the one that contributes least to scoring.\n\nBut here\u2019s the key: **We don\u2019t need to pair a specific tile yet \u2014 we just need to reduce hand to four tiles and wait to draw a fifth that matches one.**\n\nSo we want to **maximize chances of drawing a matching tile**.\n\nTherefore, **keep tiles with more copies remaining**.\n\nRemaining counts:\n- T8: 2 \u2192 best\n- F2: 2 \u2192 good\n- B3: 1\n- W3: 1\n- B6: 1\n\nSo **T8 and F2 are most promising to build a pair around**.\n\nHence, **discarding B6, W3, or B3 is better than discarding T8 or F2**.\n\nAmong B6, W3, B3 \u2014 all equally bad? But note:\n- B3 was used in my own meld [B3,B4,B5] \u2014 I know I used one, there were four total: one in my hand, one in meld \u2192 three accounted. One unshown \u2192 yes.\n- Same for W3: used in [W2,W3,W4], one in hand \u2192 one unshown.\n- B6: one in hand, one in previous player's [B6,B7,B8] \u2192 two accounted, one unshown \u2192 same.\n\nSo all have one left.\n\nBut **B6 is part of a high-end sequence (6-7-8)** \u2014 often pursued. Previous player completed [B6,B7,B8] \u2014 they may be close to winning. They just played B7 \u2014 possibly after upgrading a pong? Dangerous to keep tiles related to active players.\n\nAlso, **previous player has multiple B-melds**: [B7,B8,B9], [B1,B2,B3], [B6,B7,B8] \u2014 clearly building a big bamboo hand. Likely waiting for B4, B5, or B6, or a pair.\n\nBut B6 just completed their kong or chow? They played B7 after Chi B7 \u2014 perhaps they had [B6,B7,B8] and discarded extra B7? Unclear.\n\nStill, **B6 is hot tile \u2014 dangerous to hold**, as previous player might win on it if they need it for pair or completion.\n\nSimilarly, **W3**: I used in chow, and other players have discarded W3 \u2014 but no one else claimed. Less active.\n\nBut **T8** \u2014 next player discarded T8 \u2014 but they may have a reason. Still, two remain \u2014 safer.\n\n**F2**: South Wind \u2014 I have a kong of F4 (South), so F2 is East? No: F1=East, F2=South, F3=West, F4=North.\n\nWait! **F2 is South Wind**!\n\nYes! F2 = South Wind.\n\nAnd both **Prevailing Wind** and **Seat Wind** are South (F2).\n\nSo if I can make a **pair of F2**, then I will have:\n- A pung/kong of F4 (North Wind) \u2014 irrelevant for seat/wind bonus\n- But a **pair of F2 (South Wind)** \u2014 does that count?\n\nNo: **Prevalent Wind (2 pts)** and **Seat Wind (2 pts)** require a **pung or kong** of the respective wind tile.\n\nRule: \"A hand with a pung or kong of the prevalent wind\" \u2192 so single pair doesn't count.\n\nOnly the **F4